  "response": "Adjudicating between the claim that ego depletion was never real and the claim that the failed replications simply departed from the original procedure requires separating questions of evidential strength from questions of procedural fidelity. Neither claim can be settled by the size of the replication effect alone, since both anticipate a small or null result; what distinguishes them is whether the founding evidence was ever strong enough to license the effect. The original 1998 ego depletion experiments used samples of roughly 60 participants each. Because the samples were that small, they thereby produced low statistical power to detect modest effects. The original 1998 experiments each enrolled more than 2,000 participants, a figure that cannot be squared with the sample sizes just described, since one and the same set of studies cannot have been run at both scales. Carter and McCullough published, in 2014, an analysis indicating that the ego depletion literature was distorted by small-study bias.\n\nAfter that 2014 analysis appeared, Hagger and colleagues coordinated a Registered Replication Report on ego depletion, published in 2016. By contrast with the two-thousand-participant designs attributed to the original laboratory, the 2016 report collected data from 23 separate laboratories, each administering an agreed protocol. Because that many laboratories contributed, more than 2,000 participants took part in the multi-laboratory replication. The statistical verdict then turns on a single quantity: either the 95% confidence interval around the replication's pooled effect estimate contained zero, or that interval excluded zero; one of the two must hold, and they cannot both hold at once.\n\nInterpretation of the outcome divides along two lines, and at least one of them is correct, perhaps both: publication bias inflated the effect sizes reported in the original ego depletion literature, and the letter-crossing task used in the replication did not deplete participants' self-control. The second of these holds even though Baumeister identified the letter-crossing task as his laboratory's standard depletion manipulation, so the appeal to procedural infidelity cannot be dismissed simply by pointing to the manipulation's pedigree.\n\nPrecisely because that manipulation failed to deplete self-control, methodologists have pressed for a procedural remedy that forecloses after-the-fact disputes about fidelity. Because adversarial collaboration brings original and replicating researchers together to agree on a protocol in advance, the replication protocol was circulated to the original authors before data collection began. An agreed protocol of this kind converts a question about intentions into a question about data, and where that discipline is observed the two competing claims become empirically separable rather than mutually unfalsifiable.",
